{% decision_card number="ADR-0018" status="approved" %}
Prepublication evidence is now compared by git tree content, not commit SHA identity, resolving 2026-08-10.
{% /decision_card %}

## Context

The completion audit's `authoritative-prepublication-check` gate compares prepublication evidence (produced by `npm run ci:prepublish`) against the exact commit SHA of the candidate under review, via `validatePublicationCandidate`'s hard-fail check that `evidence.candidateSha === candidateSha`.

That equality can never survive a real merge into `main`. GitHub always server-generates a new commit SHA when a pull request is merged — whether by merge commit, squash, or rebase — even when the merged file content is byte-identical to the reviewed branch tip. So evidence generated pre-merge, bound to the pre-merge commit SHA, is structurally guaranteed to fail comparison against the post-merge `main` HEAD SHA, regardless of how sound the underlying gates were. The gate was unsatisfiable by design, not by a bug in any individual gate.

Compounding this, `evidence/publication-readiness.json` and `evidence/publication-evidence.json` are local-only, gitignored artifacts. `ci:prepublish` had never run as a required pre-merge CI check, so even setting aside the SHA-equality problem, no CI-produced evidence for a candidate existed to compare against in the first place.

## Decision drivers

- the gate must be satisfiable by a normal, real GitHub merge into `main`, not only by an artificial same-SHA condition that a merge can never produce;
- the gate must not become materially weaker in what it guarantees — it must still catch a case where the actual reviewed file content differs from what is being published;
- the fix should reuse an existing, precedented pattern in this codebase rather than invent a new content-binding mechanism;
- evidence must actually be produced pre-merge, by CI, not only by a local developer run, or the comparison has nothing authoritative to compare against.

## Options considered

This was an explicit decision made by the repository owner after investigation, not an autonomous engineering call.

1. **Rejected — relax the check to "candidate SHA is an ancestor of the merged commit."** This materially weakens the guarantee: it would also pass for a merge that manually resolved a conflict by hand-editing content differently from what was reviewed, because a manually-resolved merge commit has the reviewed commit as an ancestor regardless of what edits were made during conflict resolution. An ancestor relationship proves lineage, not that the published content matches what was evaluated. Rejected because it does not survive the exact failure mode it would need to catch.
2. **Adopted, in combination — add a real, required CI check that runs `ci:prepublish` on the pull request itself, pre-merge, uploading the evidence as a build artifact.** This produces authoritative, CI-generated evidence to compare against, rather than relying on a locally-generated file that was never CI-verified. Implemented in this decision as `.github/workflows/pr-prepublish.yml`, job id `prepublish`.
3. **Adopted, in combination — compare the git tree hash of the candidate's file content instead of the commit SHA.** A git tree object ID is a content hash of the full file tree; two commits with identical tree hashes have byte-identical file content regardless of their distinct commit identities (author, timestamp, parent, message). This is strictly not weaker than commit-SHA equality for the purpose this gate serves (detecting content drift between what was evaluated and what is published) — it is exactly the same equality class (exact match), just applied to content instead of to unrelated-to-content commit metadata. This was empirically confirmed against this repository's own merge history: commit `1658a4a` (the current `main` merge commit) and its PR-branch-tip parent `4905825` have identical tree `18ab7e93c923e309444d9aa69533c1437a317c52`, confirming that a real, zero-drift merge in this repository does produce identical tree content across distinct commit SHAs — the exact case this gate needs to tolerate. This option is also precedented by the `diffSha256` content-binding pattern already used in `src/modules/orchestration/publication/publication-policy.ts`, which compares diff content hashes rather than commit identity for a related purpose.

## Decision

Adopt options 2 and 3 in combination. Reject option 1.

`PublicationReadinessEvidenceSchema` gains a `candidateTree` field (same 40-hex-char shape as `candidateSha`, produced by `git rev-parse HEAD^{tree}`). `validatePublicationCandidate` now takes `(input, candidateSha, candidateTree)` and hard-fails when `evidence.candidateTree !== candidateTree`, rather than when `evidence.candidateSha !== candidateSha`. `candidateSha` remains in the schema and in the failure-reason message for traceability, but it is no longer the field being compared for equality.

`scripts/ci/prepublish.mjs` computes and records `candidateTree` alongside `candidateSha` when generating evidence. `scripts/ci/authorize-publication.mjs` and `scripts/agents/audit-phase-1.5.mjs` both resolve the candidate's tree hash and pass it through to `validatePublicationCandidate`.

A new workflow, `.github/workflows/pr-prepublish.yml`, runs `npm run ci:prepublish -- --base origin/main` on every pull request targeting `main` and uploads `evidence/publication-readiness.json` and `evidence/publication-evidence.json` as a build artifact, so CI — not only a local developer run — produces authoritative, checkable prepublication evidence for a candidate before merge.

## Implementation status

This decision covers code, tests, the new workflow, and documentation only. The following is explicit follow-up work, not done in this change:

- adding the `prepublish` job's check context to the `main` branch protection ruleset's `required_status_checks`;
- enabling `strict_required_status_checks_policy` ("require branches to be up to date before merging") for that ruleset.

Both require a live GitHub API mutation against the repository's ruleset, which is out of scope for this change and gated on a separate, later approval (see Risks below).

## Positive consequences

- `authoritative-prepublication-check` becomes satisfiable by a real GitHub merge, closing a structurally unsatisfiable gate without weakening what it detects;
- prepublication evidence is now produced by CI on the pull request itself, not only by an uncommitted local file, giving the gate something authoritative to compare against;
- the fix reuses this repository's existing content-hash-binding pattern (`diffSha256` in `publication-policy.ts`) rather than introducing a new mechanism;
- the rejected "ancestor of" relaxation is explicitly documented as rejected, so it is not silently reintroduced later.

## Negative consequences

- one more required field (`candidateTree`) must be kept in sync everywhere `candidateSha` is computed;
- the new `pr-prepublish.yml` workflow adds CI runtime cost to every qualifying pull request;
- until the ruleset is updated (follow-up work below), `prepublish` running in CI does not yet block a merge — it currently only produces evidence, it does not yet gate it at the GitHub level.

## Risks

- **Residual risk this change does not yet close:** tree-hash comparison against a pull request's own head can false-*pass* content that has silently drifted from `main`, or false-*fail* on unrelated `main` drift, unless GitHub's `strict_required_status_checks_policy` ("require branches up to date before merging") is also enabled for the ruleset that requires the `prepublish` context. That live ruleset mutation is explicitly out of scope for this change and requires a separate live-test-then-approval step: open a throwaway pull request, cause real drift on `main`, confirm the new `prepublish` check fails on a stale tree, sync the branch, confirm it then passes — only after that live verification should the ruleset be flipped via a `gh api` mutation, and only with separate approval.
- a tree-hash comparison, like a SHA comparison, only detects file-content drift; it cannot detect a compromised CI runner producing an evidence file for content it did not actually evaluate — this remains an assumed trust boundary of the existing evidence model, unchanged by this decision.

## Follow-up work

1. Open a throwaway pull request, cause real `main` drift while it is open, and confirm the new `prepublish` check fails against the stale candidate tree.
2. Sync that pull request's branch and confirm `prepublish` then passes.
3. Only after that live verification, and with separate approval, mutate the branch-protection ruleset: add `prepublish` to `required_status_checks` and set `strict_required_status_checks_policy` to `true`.
4. Re-run `npm run phase15:audit` once the ruleset changes land, to confirm the `agent-platform-required-check`-style gate treatment extends correctly to `prepublish`.

## Supersedes or superseded by

New decision; superseded by: none. It amends the prepublication evidence mechanism introduced under ADR-0017's platform-completion scope without superseding ADR-0017's overall phase boundary.

